body_en:
The City Council of Vitoria-Gasteiz is preparing to tackle one of the most urgent issues in today’s global landscape: the foundation of peace through territorial roots. On October 8, the Palacio Europa will host a full-day conference, *"Global Peace Through a Local Lens,"* spanning five hours. The event seeks to explore how urban centers can actively shape global stability in an increasingly fractured world.

The significance of this gathering is amplified by the participation of José Manuel Albares, Spain’s Minister of Foreign Affairs, European Union, and Cooperation, who will deliver a keynote address alongside other expert voices, institutional leaders, and civil society representatives. Together, they will examine cities’ pivotal role in addressing a "global context defined by war, humanitarian crises, rights violations, and the rise of populism and far-right extremism."

The agenda features a keynote speech followed by two roundtable discussions, addressing current conflicts, pressing challenges, and the indispensable role of local governance in fostering coexistence and peace. Leading experts will contribute to the analysis, including:
- **Rafael Heiber**, PhD in Sociology and Executive Director of the Common Action Forum;
- **Leire Moure**, specialist in International Law and International Relations;
- **Víctor Amado**, professor of Contemporary History and international relations expert;
- **Felipe Llamas**, president of Phare Territorios Globales; and
- **Alberto Alonso**, director of the Gogora Institute.

Mayor Maider Etxebarria underscored the urgency of this debate in a moment of heightened global instability. While "wars, violence, and threats to peace unfold on the international stage," she emphasized, "these crises also demand our attention as cities." Stressing the power of local action, Etxebarria affirmed that "peace starts close to home—on our streets, in our neighborhoods, and through the collective effort to build social cohesion and harmony."

This initiative, aimed at linking global diplomacy with the daily lives of citizens, was organized by Vitoria’s Coexistence and Diversity Service, reinforcing the city’s commitment to mediation and social cohesion as essential pillars of urban life.
